## Sandy Integration

### How Sandy detects a note-saving intent

**File:** `app/api/concierge/route.ts`

Sandy's system prompt needs a new instruction block. Add to the system prompt (after the page-awareness section):

```
## Note-Taking Capability
You can save notes for students. When a student says anything like:
- "keep track of this", "save this", "make a note", "remember this for me",
  "jot that down", "add to my notes"

Follow this exact flow:
1. Ask ONE clarifying question to get what you need. Use the page context first:
   - If on a course page: "Got it — shall I save this under [courseName]?"
   - If unclear which course: "Is this for [most likely course] or a different course?"
   - If no course context at all: "Got it — any particular course, or just a general note?"
2. Once you have enough context (or the student says "no course" / "just save it"),
   call the save_note action by returning a special JSON block at the END of your response:

<!--SAVE_NOTE:{"title":"<2-8 word title you generate>","content":"<the note content>","courseId":"<courseId or null>"}-->

3. Confirm naturally: "Saved! You'll find it in your notebook on the home page."

IMPORTANT: The JSON block must be the very last thing in your response, after your conversational text. Never show the raw JSON to the student.
```

### Parsing the SAVE_NOTE signal

**File:** `app/api/concierge/route.ts`

After the stream completes (in the `finally` block, similar to how chat.ts saves messages), parse for the `<!--SAVE_NOTE:...-->` pattern:

```typescript
const saveNoteMatch = fullAssistantResponse.match(/<!--SAVE_NOTE:(.*?)-->/)
if (saveNoteMatch) {
  try {
    const noteData = JSON.parse(saveNoteMatch[1])
    await prisma.studentNote.create({
      data: {
        userId: user.id,
        title: noteData.title,
        content: noteData.content,
        courseId: noteData.courseId || null,
        source: 'sandy',
      },
    })
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Failed to save note from Sandy:', e)
  }
}
```

Strip the `<!--SAVE_NOTE:...-->` block from the streamed response before it reaches the client (or strip it client-side in ConciergePanel before rendering). The student should never see the raw JSON.

**Client-side stripping in ConciergePanel:**
```typescript
// When displaying assistant message content, strip the tag:
const displayContent = msg.content.replace(/<!--SAVE_NOTE:.*?-->/s, '').trim()
```

