### Fix 15 — Cache the platform Study Buddy tool ID

**File:** `app/components/courses/CourseStudyPanel.tsx`
**Severity:** 🟡 Medium — serial fetches cause a slow initial load in the Study tab

**Implementation:**

Move the platform tool ID out of component state and into a module-level cache (a simple JS variable — no React state, no context, no backend change needed):

```typescript
// At module level, above the component:
let cachedStudyBuddyToolId: string | null | undefined = undefined
// undefined = not yet fetched, null = fetched but not found, string = found
```

In the component, update the `useEffect`:
```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  // If already cached (even null), skip the fetch
  if (cachedStudyBuddyToolId !== undefined) {
    setStudyBuddyToolId(cachedStudyBuddyToolId)
    setLoadingTool(false)
    return
  }

  fetch('/api/tools?toolType=STUDY_BUDDY&limit=1', {
    headers: { 'x-demo-user-email': userEmail },
  })
    .then(r => r.ok ? r.json() : null)
    .then(data => {
      const tools = data?.tools ?? data ?? []
      const id = tools.length > 0 ? tools[0].id : null
      cachedStudyBuddyToolId = id  // write to module cache
      setStudyBuddyToolId(id)
    })
    .catch(() => {
      cachedStudyBuddyToolId = null
      setStudyBuddyToolId(null)
    })
    .finally(() => setLoadingTool(false))
}, [userEmail])
```

The first tab visit fetches once. Every subsequent visit to the Study tab (including after tab switches) shows instantly. The cache lives for the browser session — page reload re-fetches once.

