---

## Phase 4 — New Feature: Course Setup Wizard

**What to build:** When an educator creates a new course (currently just drops them on an empty materials tab), intercept with a 3-step setup experience shown in a modal or dedicated panel.

**Steps:**
1. **Import Syllabus** — "Upload your syllabus PDF to scaffold the course automatically" (calls 1-B). Skip button available.
2. **Review & adjust** — Show the created materials list inline with edit affordances. "Add more" opens the material form. Skip if no materials were imported.
3. **Create AI Teaching Assistant** — Show the `CourseMagicButton` logic inline: "Your materials are in. Create an AI TA that knows your course?" Accept / Skip.

**Implementation approach:**

Add a `showSetupWizard` boolean state. Set it to `true` immediately after `handleCreateCourse` succeeds. The wizard renders as a full-panel overlay on the course detail area (not a browser modal) — a white card with a step indicator, replacing the normal tab content.

Each "Next" advances the step. "Skip" advances without action. After step 3 (or any final skip), set `showSetupWizard = false` and show the normal tab interface.

This wizard replaces the current behavior where Sandy auto-fires "Tell me how to get started with this course." — remove that auto-prefill trigger (around line 1140) once the wizard is in place.

---

## Phase 5 — AI Integration: Discussion Prompt Generator

**What to build:** A "Generate discussion prompt" button in the Discussion tab header. Clicking it calls an AI endpoint that looks at the course materials and recent Pulse data to suggest 3 discussion thread ideas.

**New API route:** `app/api/courses/[id]/discussions/suggest/route.ts`

```
POST /api/courses/[id]/discussions/suggest
Header: x-demo-user-email

Response 200:
{
  suggestions: Array<{
    title: string,
    content: string,
    targetModule: number | null
  }>
}
```

AI prompt (use Haiku):
```
You are a university instructor's assistant. Given the course materials below, suggest 3 high-quality discussion thread prompts that would spark meaningful student engagement.

For each prompt:
- Make it open-ended (no single correct answer)
- Ground it in a specific concept from the materials
- Be concise (title under 10 words, content 2–3 sentences)

Return raw JSON array:
[{"title": "...", "content": "...", "targetModule": number|null}]

COURSE MATERIALS SUMMARY:
{materialTitles joined by newline}
```

**UI changes in `app/courses/page.tsx` — Discussion tab:**

Add a "Get prompt ideas" button (Sparkles icon, blue outline style) in the discussion header, next to "New Thread." Clicking it:
1. Calls the suggest endpoint.
2. Shows 3 suggestion cards above the thread list, each with "Use this prompt" (pre-fills the New Thread form and opens it) and "Dismiss."

New state:
```ts
const [discussionSuggestions, setDiscussionSuggestions] = useState<DiscussionSuggestion[]>([])
const [loadingDiscussionSuggestions, setLoadingDiscussionSuggestions] = useState(false)
```

---

## Implementation Order & Notes

### Recommended sequence:
1. **1-D** (replace alerts/confirms) — small, touches everything, makes all subsequent work cleaner.
2. **1-C** (material editing) — backend is a one-line extension; frontend is the inline form.
3. **1-A** (PDF upload for materials) — new route + file input in the existing form.
4. **2-D** (prevent duplicate AI TA) — 10-line backend change.
5. **2-B** (relabel "Use form") — one line.
6. **3-A** (hide Study tab from educators) — one filter line.
7. **3-D** (hide Submissions tab for non-seeded courses) — one filter line.
8. **1-B** (syllabus import) — new route with AI call + UI card.
9. **3-E** (danger zone in settings) — new UI section + DELETE route.
10. **3-C** (Pulse actionability) — add action links to warn modules.
11. **3-B** (Discussion markdown + unread badge).
12. **2-A** (close tool loop with refresh button).
13. **2-C** (tool ordering — requires schema migration).
14. **4** (setup wizard — larger, builds on 1-A and 1-B being done).
15. **5** (discussion prompt generator — nice-to-have, needs 1-B done first for good material context).

### Key constraints to remember:
- All API routes use `x-demo-user-email` header for auth — no session/JWT on the server.
- Prisma client must use `PrismaPg` adapter (see `app/lib/prisma.ts` for the pattern — do not create a raw `new PrismaClient()`).
- Tailwind v4 — no `tailwind.config.js` class allowlisting needed; JIT is always on.
- UK blue is `#0033A0` / `#002580` (hover). Match existing button patterns exactly.
- No `window.alert()` or `window.confirm()` — use inline UI patterns.
- No new dependencies unless absolutely necessary. `pdf-parse` and `@anthropic-ai/sdk` are already installed.
- The `app/courses/page.tsx` file is ~3,400 lines. When editing, read the specific section first before making changes to avoid losing context.

---

## Acceptance Criteria Summary

| Item | Done when... |
|------|-------------|
| 1-A PDF upload | Educator can upload a PDF; content populates the material textarea; educator saves; material appears in list |
| 1-B Syllabus import | Educator on empty course uploads a PDF syllabus; N materials are created with module numbers; list refreshes |
| 1-C Material editing | Any saved material has an Edit button; clicking it opens an inline form pre-filled; saving updates the material in place |
| 1-D No more alert/confirm | Zero `window.alert()` and zero `window.confirm()` calls remain in `app/courses/page.tsx` |
| 2-A Tool loop | After building a tool with the Builder and returning to course, one click links it |
| 2-B Label fix | "Use form" button reads "Create manually" |
| 2-C Tool ordering | Tools tab shows up/down arrows; reordering persists on refresh |
| 2-D No duplicate TA | Clicking "Create AI Teaching Assistant" twice creates exactly one bot |
| 3-A Study tab hidden | Educator tab bar does not show Study tab |
| 3-B Markdown + badge | Discussion posts render markdown; a red dot appears on the Discussion tab when unread threads exist |
| 3-C Pulse actionability | Struggling modules show "Build a tool for this module" link |
| 3-D Submissions hidden | Non-seeded courses show no Submissions tab |
| 3-E Danger zone | Settings tab has a delete course section with two-step inline confirmation |
| 4 Setup wizard | New course creation walks educator through 3-step setup modal |
| 5 Discussion AI | Discussion tab has "Get prompt ideas" that generates 3 thread suggestions |
</file>

<file path="EDUCATOR_TOOL_BUILDER_UX_FIXES.md">
# Educator Tool Builder — UX Fixes

**Source:** Walkthrough audit of the educator tool-creation flow
**Scenario tested:** Law professor building a Socratic hearsay exception tool for LAW 304, Module 7
**Priority:** P1 = blocks quality output · P2 = causes drop-off · P3 = polish

---

## P1 — System Prompt Gets No Help

**Problem:** The system prompt is the most important field in the entire form — every student interaction depends on it — yet it gets a blank 7-row textarea and one generic example (biochemistry tutor). Most educators have never written a prompt before and will write 2-3 sentences that produce a mediocre tool.

**Files:**
- `app/publish/page.tsx` — Step 3 system prompt section (~lines 882-950)
- `app/api/build-tool/route.ts` — reuse this endpoint

**Changes:**

1. Add a **"Generate with AI"** button next to the System Prompt label. On click, open a small modal:
   - Input: "Describe what you want students to do in one sentence"
   - On submit, call `POST /api/build-tool` with a targeted generation prompt:
     ```
     Generate ONLY a system prompt (no other text) for a {subType} tool called "{name}".
     Goal: {userOneliner}
     Audience: {intendedAudience or 'university students'}
     Structure it as: persona → behavior rules → Socratic constraints → what NOT to do.
     Output only the system prompt text, ready to paste.
     ```
   - Stream the result directly into the system prompt textarea.

2. Add a **template picker** above the textarea: a row of 4-5 pills — "Socratic Tutor," "Mock Interview," "Case Analyzer," "Writing Coach," "Quiz" — each pre-populating the textarea with a complete, well-structured template. Templates live in a new `app/lib/system-prompt-templates.ts` file:

   ```ts
   export const SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATES: Record<string, string> = {
     SOCRATIC: `You are a Socratic tutor for {subject}. Never give the answer directly...`,
     MOCK_INTERVIEW: `You are a realistic interviewer for {role}. Stay in character...`,
     CASE_ANALYZER: `You are a case analysis coach. Present one scenario at a time...`,
     WRITING_COACH: `You are a writing coach. When a student pastes their draft...`,
     QUIZ: `You are a quiz tutor. Present one question at a time...`,
   }
   ```

3. Below the textarea, add a **character count hint**: "Most effective prompts are 200–600 characters. Yours: {n}." Show green for 200-600, yellow for under 100, gray otherwise.

---

## P1 — No Mandatory "Test Before Publish"

**Problem:** The live chat preview in Step 5 is optional and placed *below* the publish button. Most educators click Publish without ever testing, meaning tools with broken or vague system prompts go live to students immediately.

**Files:**
- `app/publish/page.tsx` — Step 5 section (~lines 1423-1599)
- `app/components/publish/ToolPreviewChat.tsx`

**Changes:**

1. Move the **ToolPreviewChat** component *above* the publication options in Step 5. Rename the button from "Test this tool first" to "Preview as a student →" and give it more visual prominence (solid button, not ghost).

2. Track whether the educator has sent at least one message in the preview chat. Add state:
   ```ts
   const [hasTested, setHasTested] = useState(false)
   ```
   When the preview chat receives its first user message, set `hasTested = true`.

3. Disable the "Publish Experience" button until `hasTested === true`. Show a tooltip on hover when disabled: "Send at least one message in the preview above to make sure your tool is working the way you expect."

4. *(Optional, softer version)* If you don't want a hard gate, show a yellow warning banner instead: "⚠ You haven't tested this tool yet. We recommend sending a few messages above before publishing."

---

## P1 — Builder → Form Pre-fill Has Silent Failure

**Problem:** If the conversational builder's spec extraction fails (malformed JSON in `<!--SPEC:...-->`, network hiccup, truncated stream), the educator arrives at `/publish` with an empty form. They think the builder worked — the chat completed — but the form is blank. No error, no feedback.

**Files:**
- `app/publish/page.tsx` — `useEffect` that reads `searchParams.get('prefill')` on mount
- `app/components/BuilderChatPanel.tsx` — spec extraction logic

**Changes:**

1. In `/publish`, when a `?prefill=` or `?session=` param is present, attempt to parse it and immediately check if the extracted fields are meaningfully populated (at minimum: `name` and `systemPrompt` non-empty).

2. If parsing fails or critical fields are empty, show a dismissable banner at the top of the form:
   ```
   ⚠ We had trouble pre-filling your form from the builder conversation.
   [View what we captured ↓]  [Re-open builder →]
   ```
   "View what we captured" expands a `<pre>` block with the raw extracted JSON so the educator can copy-paste manually. "Re-open builder" links back to `/builder?session={id}` to resume the conversation.

3. In `BuilderChatPanel.tsx`, before redirecting to `/publish`, validate that the spec contains at minimum `name`, `shortDescription`, and `systemPrompt`. If any are missing, don't redirect — instead display an inline warning: "Your tool is almost ready. I still need [field list] before we can move to the publish form."

---

## P2 — Three Entry Paths With No Hierarchy

**Problem:** The Build Hub presents three creation paths simultaneously — the conversational hero text box, the Experience Gallery, and the "Publish Tool" nav link — with no explanation of when to use each. First-time educators face cognitive overload before building anything.

**Files:**
- `app/build/page.tsx` — top section of the Build Hub page

**Changes:**

1. Replace the current hero section with a **3-panel path chooser**:

   ```
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  How do you want to build?                          │
   ├─────────────┬─────────────────┬────────────────────┤
   │ 🧭 Template │ 💬 Describe it  │ ✏ Fill out a form  │
   │             │                 │                    │
   │ Browse 30+  │ Tell the AI     │ Step-by-step form  │
   │ experience  │ what you want   │ with full control  │
   │ types and   │ in plain        │ over all settings  │
   │ customize   │ English         │                    │
   │             │                 │                    │
   │ [Browse →]  │ [Start chat →]  │ [Open form →]      │
   └─────────────┴─────────────────┴────────────────────┘
   ```

2. The "Describe it" panel expands inline to show the text input (don't navigate away). The "Template" panel scrolls down to the existing Experience Gallery. "Fill out a form" navigates to `/publish`.

3. Collapse the conversational text input to be hidden by default, revealed only after choosing "Describe it." This removes the ambiguity of the hero-plus-gallery layout.

---

## P2 — No Post-Publish "What's Next"

**Problem:** After clicking "Publish Experience," the educator is redirected to the tool detail page with no guidance on what to do next. There's no obvious "How do I share this with students?" answer, no course-linking prompt, no analytics path.

**Files:**
- `app/publish/page.tsx` — `handleSubmit` redirect after successful POST
- `app/tools/[id]/page.tsx` — tool detail page

**Changes:**

1. After a successful tool creation (`POST /api/tools` returns 201), instead of a plain redirect to `/tools/${id}`, navigate to `/tools/${id}?published=new`.

2. In `app/tools/[id]/page.tsx`, detect `?published=new` on mount and show a **post-publish success card** as a modal overlay or prominent top banner:

   ```
   ✅ Your tool is live!

   [📋 Copy student link]   [📚 Add to a course]   [📊 View analytics]

   Students can find it in the marketplace under "{category}" or via the direct link above.
   ```

   - "Copy student link" copies `https://thesandbox.uky.edu/tools/{id}` to clipboard.
   - "Add to a course" opens a dropdown of the educator's courses; on select, calls `PATCH /api/tools/{id}` to link `linkedCourseCode`.
   - "View analytics" scrolls to or navigates to the analytics tab.

3. Dismiss the card and strip the `?published=new` param from the URL on any interaction or after 10 seconds.

---

## P2 — Tool Sub-Type Lost in Form Wizard

**Problem:** The Prisma schema has 7 distinct tool types (`CHATBOT`, `SIMULATION`, `QUIZ`, `AI_INTERVIEW`, `DEBATE`, `STUDY_BUDDY`, `SIMULATION`) and the builder at `/builder` surfaces 8 well-described experience types. But the publish form collapses all of these into a single "CHATBOT" option. An educator who wants a "Debate Partner" or "Mock Interview" has no way to express that through the form.

**Files:**
- `app/publish/page.tsx` — Step 2 tool type selection
- `app/lib/types.ts` — `ToolType` enum reference
- `app/publish/page.tsx` — `getStarterSystemPrompt()` function (already exists, already has templates per type)

**Changes:**

1. In Step 2, after the educator selects "Conversational AI," show a second row: **"What kind of experience is this?"** — render the 8 cards from `BuilderChatPanel`'s `TOOL_TYPES` array as selectable tiles with icons and one-line descriptions.

2. Selecting a sub-type sets a new `form.experienceSubType` field (no schema change needed — this is metadata used only in the form to drive defaults, then mapped to the `toolType` enum on submit).

3. On sub-type selection, auto-populate the system prompt with the existing `getStarterSystemPrompt(subType)` template *only if the field is currently empty*. Show a toast: "We've pre-filled a starter system prompt for a {subType}. Edit it to match your course."

4. Also update the Persona Name placeholder to be contextually relevant (e.g., "Judge," "Coach," "Interviewer") based on sub-type. Don't overwrite if the educator has already typed something.

---

## P2 — Welcome Message and Starter Questions Get No Help

**Problem:** Both fields are blank by default with no guidance. The welcome message is the first thing students see; blank means a generic or missing greeting. Starter questions are high-value affordances that most educators leave empty because they don't know what effective ones look like.

**Files:**
- `app/publish/page.tsx` — Step 3 welcome message and starter questions sections

**Changes:**

1. **Welcome Message:** Generate a default welcome message as placeholder text (not pre-filled value, so saving is always intentional) based on `personaName` + `name` + first learning objective if present. Example placeholder:
   ```
   Hi! I'm {personaName}, your guide for {toolName}.
   Tell me about a fact pattern you're working through, or pick one of the questions below to get started.
   ```
   Update the placeholder in real time as those fields change.

2. **Starter Questions:** Add a **"Suggest questions"** button (small, ghost style) that fires a short `POST /api/build-tool` call:
   ```
   Generate exactly 4 starter questions a student might click when opening a {subType} tool
   about "{systemPromptSummary}". Make them concrete, short (under 12 words), and pedagogically
   useful. Return as a JSON array of strings only.
   ```
   Parse the response and populate the 4 inputs. Let the educator edit freely after.

---

## P3 — Custom Metrics Are Incomprehensible

**Problem:** The metric type dropdown shows `COUNTER | DURATION | RATING | BOOLEAN | TEXT` — enum values that mean nothing to a non-engineer. Educators have no idea what they produce or how students interact with them.

**Files:**
- `app/publish/page.tsx` — Step 4 custom metrics section
- `METRIC_TYPE_LABELS` constant (line 40) already has friendlier labels but they're only used in the dropdown, not explained

**Changes:**

1. Replace the bare enum dropdown with a **visual type picker**: cards or a segmented control, each with a label, icon, and one-line example:

   | Type | Label | Example |
   |------|-------|---------|
   | COUNTER | Count | "Times student asked for a hint" |
   | DURATION | Time spent | "Minutes in active conversation" |
   | RATING | Score (1-10) | "Student self-rates confidence after session" |
   | BOOLEAN | Yes / No | "Did student complete all 5 cases?" |
   | TEXT | AI note | "Key concept the student struggled with" |

2. Below each added metric, show a one-line preview: *"In analytics, you'll see: [Metric Name] — [example value]."*

---

## P3 — Gamification Config Has No Preview

**Problem:** The `totalSteps` and `stepLabel` fields are fully abstract. Entering "5" and "Case" means nothing visually. Educators don't know what students will see.

**Files:**
- `app/publish/page.tsx` — Step 4 gamification section

**Changes:**

1. Directly below the gamification inputs, render a **live inline preview** of the progress bar as the educator types:

   ```
   Preview: ● ● ○ ○ ○   Case 2 of 5
   ```

   Use the actual `stepLabel` value and `totalSteps` to render 5 dots (filled/empty) and the label text. Update in real time. This makes the abstract config immediately concrete.

---

## P3 — Approval Status Is Unexplained

**Problem:** When an educator publishes and their tool gets `COMMUNITY` status, there's no explanation of what this means vs `APPROVED`. They see no checkmark and don't know if something went wrong or if they need to take an action.

**Files:**
- `app/publish/page.tsx` — Step 5 publication options

**Changes:**

1. In Step 5, next to the "Publish Experience" button, add a one-line status explainer:

   > Your tool will be live as **Community** — visible to all students, no review required.
   > Want a **Verified** badge? [Request admin review] — usually approved within 1-2 days.

2. The "Request admin review" link sets the existing checkbox that triggers `PENDING` status (already built — just needs to be surfaced with this framing instead of being a bare checkbox at the bottom of the page).

---

## P3 — System Prompt Quick-Edit After Publishing

**Problem:** Iterating on a system prompt requires navigating to `/publish?edit=[id]`, going through the 5-step wizard back to Step 3, editing, and re-publishing. This is too much friction for what is the most-iterated-upon field in the entire product.

**Files:**
- `app/tools/[id]/page.tsx` — tool detail page, educator view

**Changes:**

1. On the tool detail page, when the current user is the creator, add a small **"Edit system prompt"** link in the configuration section (or as a dropdown option on the settings menu).

2. Clicking it opens an inline edit panel (not a modal, not a full page): the textarea expands in place with Save/Cancel buttons. On Save, `PATCH /api/tools/{id}` with just `{ systemPrompt: newValue }`.

3. After save, show a toast: "System prompt updated. Test it with a student message →" with a link to the chat tab on the same page.

---

## Implementation Order

| # | Change | Effort | Impact |
|---|--------|--------|--------|
| 1 | Sub-type picker in Step 2 + auto-fill system prompt template | S | P1 |
| 2 | "Generate with AI" button on system prompt field | M | P1 |
| 3 | Mandatory test-before-publish gate | S | P1 |
| 4 | Post-publish "what's next" success card | S | P2 |
| 5 | Welcome message placeholder auto-generation | XS | P2 |
| 6 | "Suggest starter questions" AI button | S | P2 |
| 7 | Builder → form pre-fill failure banner | S | P1 |
| 8 | 3-panel path chooser on Build Hub | M | P2 |
| 9 | Gamification live preview | XS | P3 |
| 10 | Custom metrics visual type picker | S | P3 |
| 11 | Approval status explainer in Step 5 | XS | P3 |
| 12 | System prompt quick-edit on tool detail page | M | P3 |
</file>

