# Marketplace UX v2 — Architecture & Implementation Spec
### The Sandbox / CATS-AI
### Principal Architect Brief — March 2026

This document translates the marketplace UX audit into a complete, prioritized implementation plan. Each section identifies the problem, the architectural decision, the exact files to change, and the implementation instructions. Work items are ordered by user impact per effort.

---

## Guiding Principles

1. **Reduce cognitive load at every scroll depth.** The page currently asks users to process 4 distinct sections before they can search. First impressions should be fast, scannable, and role-aware.
2. **Trust is earned top-to-bottom.** Curated content appears first. Community content is opt-in. Never default to showing unverified work.
3. **Cards must communicate enough to motivate a click.** Emoji + name + description is the floor, not the ceiling. Tool type is the single most valuable differentiator and must be visible at a glance.
4. **The page must behave differently for a first-time visitor vs a returning user.** Personalization starts with role and department, not a login wall.
5. **Naming must be user-facing, not system-facing.** Internal product names (League Engine, Sandcastle, Study Buddy) must be translated to plain language at every user-visible surface.

---

## Change 1 — Rename the Top-Level Tabs

**Priority:** P1
**Effort:** XS
**Files:** `app/tools/page.tsx`

### Problem
The current tab labels "Educational / University / Live" do not map to user mental models. "Educational" and "University" sound synonymous. "Live" implies real-time video, not gamified experiences.

### Decision
Replace with intent-based labels that describe what the user *does*, not what the system *contains*.

| Old | New | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Educational | Learn | Active, student-facing, implies tools for study |
| University | Services | Signals official support — not an academic category |
| Live | Play | Honest about the experience type; differentiates from Learn |

### Implementation
In `app/tools/page.tsx`, update the `TABS` constant:

```ts
const TABS = [
  { key: 'educational', label: 'Learn' },
  { key: 'university', label: 'Services' },
  { key: 'live', label: 'Play' },
] as const
```

Add an icon to each tab to reinforce the label at a glance. Import `BookOpen`, `Building2`, and `Gamepad2` from lucide-react (Building2 and Gamepad2 already imported). Render them inside the tab `Link`:

```tsx
// icon map alongside TABS
const TAB_ICONS = {
  educational: BookOpen,
  university:  Building2,
  live:        Gamepad2,
}
```

Render each tab as: `<Icon className="w-3.5 h-3.5 inline-block mr-1.5" />{tab.label}`

---

## Change 2 — Move Search to the Top of the Page

**Priority:** P1
**Effort:** S
**Files:** `app/tools/page.tsx`

### Problem
The search bar is buried below the page header, the featured section, and two collection strips. A user with explicit search intent has to scroll ~600px before they can type. This is the single largest friction point on the page.

### Decision
The search bar moves into the **page header**, directly below the tab navigation. It becomes always-visible on the Learn tab regardless of scroll position. The current in-body search area is removed.

### Implementation

**Step 1 — Move the search `<div>` block** (currently inside the Browse All section, lines ~560–620) up to directly below the tab `<div>` inside the page header. It should sit inside `max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8` but below the tab row.

**Step 2 — Remove the old search block** from the Learn tab's Browse section. The filter bar (category pills, sort, Advanced Filters) stays where it is — it's a browsing tool, not a search entry point.

**Step 3 — Update "Ask Sandy →"** from an inline text link to a proper button:

```tsx
<button
  type="button"
  onClick={...sandyPrefill...}
  className="flex items-center gap-1 text-xs font-semibold text-[#0033A0] border border-[#0033A0]/30 rounded-full px-3 py-1 hover:bg-blue-50 transition-colors"
>
  <Sparkles className="w-3 h-3" />
  Ask Sandy
</button>
```

Place this button to the right of the search input as a sibling, not below it.

**Step 4 — Wire scroll-to-browse.** The search results grid is still in the Browse section at the bottom. When the user types in the header search, the page should scroll the Browse grid into view automatically:

```ts
// Inside the setSearch effect:
if (value.trim()) browseRef.current?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' })
```

---

## Change 3 — Default Browse Tab to "Verified Only"

**Priority:** P1
**Effort:** XS
**Files:** `app/tools/page.tsx`

### Problem
Defaulting to "All Tools" (community/unverified) means the first browsing experience shows potentially low-quality content. This erodes trust, especially for administrators and educators evaluating the platform.

### Decision
Flip the default. Verified-only is the landing state. Community tools are the opt-in.

### Implementation
Change the `useState` initialization from `'community'` to `'marketplace'`:

```ts
const [activeTab, setActiveTab] = useState<'marketplace' | 'community'>('marketplace')
```

Rename the display labels from "Verified Only / All Tools" to "**Curated / Community**" to reduce the implied quality judgement on community tools while still maintaining the hierarchy:

```tsx
{tab === 'marketplace' ? 'Curated' : 'Community'}
```

---

## Change 4 — Add Tool Type Pill to Compact Cards

**Priority:** P1
**Effort:** S
**Files:** `app/tools/page.tsx` (the `SimpleToolCard` component)

### Problem
At 4 columns with only name + description, all compact tools look identical in visual weight. A user can't quickly differentiate "AI Chat" from "Practice Quiz" from "Scenario" without reading every card. This kills browsing efficiency.

### Decision
Add a small tool-type pill to each `SimpleToolCard`. It appears below the title, above the description. It uses the same color system as the category text.

### Implementation

**Step 1 — Create a user-facing type label map** in `page.tsx`:

```ts
const TOOL_TYPE_LABELS: Record<string, string> = {
  CHATBOT:      'AI Chat',
  SIMULATION:   'Scenario',
  QUIZ:         'Practice Quiz',
  AI_INTERVIEW: 'Mock Interview',
  DEBATE:       'Debate',
  STUDY_BUDDY:  'Study Aid',
  EXTERNAL:     'External Link',
}
```

**Step 2 — Add to `SimpleToolCard` JSX** between the title and description:

```tsx
{tool.toolType && TOOL_TYPE_LABELS[tool.toolType] && (
